<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achirimbi II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achirimbi II was Fon of Bafut, a kingdom in the Grassfields of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, from 1932 to 1968. He ruled under British administration and into independent Cameroon. He became widely known abroad through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-durrell">Gerald Durrell</a>'s book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bafut-beagles">The Bafut Beagles</a> (1954), which described Durrell's animal-collecting stay at his court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achirimbi-ii.jpg" alt="Achirimbi II" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograaf / photographer: Drs. G.W. (Gerrit Willem) Grootenhuis (1930) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_De_Fon_van_Bafut_poseert_bij_de_mierenval_op_zijn_erf_TMnr_20012968.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
